Chapter: CamCapture
  cites Works chapter GopXhci
  cites Works chapter GopUsbCam
  grounds Device.Port
  grounds Device.Mmio

 This is the implementation of the Camera effect.

 capture and capture-raw were declared as operations of the Camera
 effect in Foreword chapter Camera with no handler, so a call died at
 emit with CDX2040. They are real here, over the isochronous endpoint
 Works chapter GopUsbCam drives: a frame that arrives is a frame the
 controller delivered, not a constant. Unlike Location and Sensors,
 nothing about this chapter is a placeholder.

 They are not in the foreword because reading the camera means resetting
 a root port, enumerating a device, walking a configuration descriptor
 and queueing an isochronous TRB, and none of that is a foreword
 concern. Device.Port and Device.Mmio are grounded here so a caller
 declares Camera and nothing else, which is the same arrangement Kernel
 chapter MicInput uses for the microphone.

Section: One Format, Refused Rather Than Resized

 capture-raw takes a width and a height because the effect declared it
 that way. This device publishes exactly one format, 160 by 120 YUYV,
 and this chapter refuses any other size instead of scaling to it.

 That is the load-bearing decision in the chapter, so it is worth the
 sentence. A resize would answer every request successfully while
 quietly changing what the sensor saw, and a caller asking for 640 by
 480 would receive an upscaled 160 by 120 frame with no way to tell.
 Refusing is the answer a caller can act on: cf-ok is False and the
 dimensions it can have are readable from cam-frame-width and
 cam-frame-height.

Section: The Captured Frame

 A frame is the buffer plus the shape of what is in it, so a caller
 reads pixels rather than an address, and a failed capture is a value
 rather than a convention about a sentinel.

  CamFrame = record {
    cf-ok : Boolean,
    cf-width : Integer,
    cf-height : Integer,
    cf-cam : UsbCam
  }

  cam-no-frame : CamFrame
  cam-no-frame = CamFrame { cf-ok = False, cf-width = 0, cf-height = 0, cf-cam = cam-no-cam }

 The camera sits on root port 3, which is PORTSC index 2: the slot
 context turns the zero-based index into the one-based root port number.

  cam-port-index : Integer = 2
  cam-cfg-descriptor-bytes : Integer = 256

Section: Capture

 The sequence is the one codex/test/apps/usb-cam-frame proves: connect
 to the controller, reset the port, open the device, fetch its
 configuration descriptor, find the streaming interface at the alternate
 setting that HAS an endpoint, open that endpoint, queue one frame.

 Every step answers cam-no-frame on failure rather than a partly-built
 frame, because a CamFrame whose cf-ok is True must mean the pixels were
 delivered. cam-read-frame answers zero for success and a negative for a
 timeout, and the difference matters: a timeout leaves the buffer
 holding whatever it held before, which would read as a picture.

  cam-capture-frame : [Camera] CamFrame
  cam-capture-frame = act
    xh <- xhci-connect
    if xh.xh-ok == False then cam-no-frame
    else let speed = xhci-reset-port xh cam-port-index
    in let dev = xhci-open-device xh cam-port-index speed
    in if dev.ud-ok == False then cam-no-frame
    else let cfg = alloc-zeroed cam-cfg-descriptor-bytes 64
    in let got = usb-get-descriptor xh dev 2 0 cfg (cam-cfg-descriptor-bytes - 1)
    in if got == False then cam-no-frame
    else cam-capture-configured xh dev cfg
  end

  cam-capture-configured : XhciHost, UsbDev, Integer -> [Camera] CamFrame
  cam-capture-configured (xh) (dev) (cfg) =
    let total = cam-cfg-total cfg
    in let iface = cam-find-stream-iface cfg 0 total
    in if iface < 0 then cam-no-frame
    else let cam = cam-open xh dev cfg iface total
    in if cam.uc-ok == False then cam-no-frame
    else if cam-read-frame cam /= 0 then cam-no-frame
    else CamFrame {
      cf-ok = True,
      cf-width = cam-frame-width,
      cf-height = cam-frame-height,
      cf-cam = cam
    }

Section: What A Capture Costs

 Every call does the whole sequence: reset, enumerate, descriptor,
 configure, one frame. That is what an effect operation taking no handle
 forces -- capture's declared shape has nowhere to keep a camera between
 calls -- and it is honest about the price rather than caching a device
 behind the caller's back.

 The price is worth naming because this heap has no collector. Each call
 allocates a 256-byte descriptor buffer and a 38400-byte frame buffer,
 and both live until the function that called capture returns. Three
 captures in one function is a little over a hundred kilobytes still
 held; a loop taking a frame a second is a leak with a camera attached
 to it, which is the phrase GopUsbCam already uses about the per-frame
 allocation it was written to avoid.

 So a caller that wants ONE frame uses capture, and a caller that wants a
 STREAM does not: it cites Works chapter GopUsbCam, holds the UsbCam
 itself, and calls cam-read-frame, which reuses the buffer opened once.
 The effect is the convenient door, not the fast one.

Section: The Effect Operations

  capture : [Camera] CamFrame
  capture = cam-capture-frame

  capture-raw : Integer, Integer -> [Camera] CamFrame
  capture-raw (w) (h) =
    if w /= cam-frame-width then cam-no-frame
    else if h /= cam-frame-height then cam-no-frame
    else cam-capture-frame

Section: Reading Pixels

 YUYV packs one luma byte per pixel and shares chroma across a pair.
 A frame that is not ok answers zero everywhere rather than reading an
 address it does not own.

  frame-luma : CamFrame, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  frame-luma (f) (x) (y) =
    if f.cf-ok == False then 0
    else if x < 0 then 0
    else if y < 0 then 0
    else if x >= f.cf-width then 0
    else if y >= f.cf-height then 0
    else cam-luma (f.cf-cam) x y

  frame-chroma : CamFrame, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  frame-chroma (f) (x) (y) =
    if f.cf-ok == False then 0
    else if x < 0 then 0
    else if y < 0 then 0
    else if x >= f.cf-width then 0
    else if y >= f.cf-height then 0
    else cam-chroma (f.cf-cam) x y
